A group $G$ is _finite_ if $|G| < oo$. $|G|$ is called the _order_ of $G$.

*Remark:*

(i) $H subset G$ subgroup $=> |H| < oo$
$ G = union.big_(g in G\/H) g H, quad "multiplication is bijection" => |g H| = |H| $
$ => |G| = |G\/H| |H| $
$|G\/H| = [G : H]$ is sometimes called the _index_ of $H$ in $G$.

Orders of subgroups divide the order of the group! [Lagrange's theorem]

(ii) $g in G$: $Gamma_g := {g^i | i in ZZ} subset G$, $|Gamma_g| < oo$
$
  & => exists n, m: g^n = g^m => exists k in NN: g^k = e \
  & => Gamma_g = {e, g, g^2, ..., g^k = e} tilde.equiv ZZ_k
$
$ k =: "ord"(g) = "ord"(Gamma_g) $
Lagrange theorem $=> "ord"(g) | |G|$.

What does this mean if $|G|$ is prime?
$ => "ord"(g) = |G| quad forall e != g in G quad => quad #box(stroke: 0.5pt, inset: 4pt, $G tilde.equiv ZZ_(|G|)$) $

*Examples:*
+ $ZZ_(p q) supset ZZ_p$ generated by $[q]$, \
  $ZZ_(p q) supset ZZ_q$ generated by $[p]$
+ $|D_n| = 2n$:
  $
    Gamma_(S_i) & = {e = R_0, S_i} subset D_n, \
    ZZ_n & = {R_0, ..., R_(n-1)} = Gamma_(R_1) subset D_n,
  $
  $ Gamma_(R_2) tilde.equiv Gamma_(R_1), space n "odd", quad quad n = p q, space Gamma_(R_p) tilde.equiv ZZ_q $

