_Note:_ since multiplication is a bijection in $G$ $=>$ all rows (columns) are permutations of the first row (column).
$ g: g_i |-> g g_i = g_(pi_g (i)), quad pi_g in S_n $
$
  h(g g_i) & = h(g_(pi_g (i))) = g_(pi_h compose pi_g (i)) \
  #box(width: 3.2em, align(center, rotate(90deg, $=$))) & \
  (h g) g_i & = g_(pi_(h g) (i))
$
$ => pi: g |-> pi_g "is a group homomorphism" G -> S_n $
$ pi_g = e in S_n => pi_g (i) = i space forall i => g = e $
$ -> pi: G arrow.r.hook S_n "is injective" $
$ => G tilde.equiv pi(G) subset S_n quad "all groups of order" n "are subgroups of" S_n "!" $

*Importance of $S_n$ for physics:*
- statistics of particles in quantum systems.
- representations of $S_n$ govern the representation theory of $"SU"(N)$.

*Symmetric group:*
$ S_n := "Bij"(S = {1, ..., n}), quad |S_n| = n! $

Notation:
$
  #perm-fig((1, 3, 0, 3.7, none, 5), ("1", "2", "3", "4", "...", "n")) quad #aside[draw a row towards where the number goes]
$
or
$ pi = mat(delim: "(", 1, 2, 3, dots, n; pi(1), pi(2), pi(3), dots, pi(n)) $

*Example:* $S_4 in.rev pi$ cyclic permutation of $(1, 2, 3, 4)$:
$ pi = mat(delim: "(", 1, 2, 3, 4; 2, 3, 4, 1) = #perm-fig((1, 2, 3, 0), ("1", "2", "3", "4")) $

*Example:* What is a Cayley subgroup of $ZZ_4$?
$ g_i = [i], quad g_0 = e, quad g_1 = [1], quad g_2 = [2], quad g_3 = [3] $
$ pi: ZZ_4 -> S_4, quad g_i g_j = g_(i + j mod 4) $
$
  pi_e & = mat(delim: "(", 1, 2, 3, 4; 1, 2, 3, 4), quad && pi_([1]) = mat(delim: "(", 1, 2, 3, 4; 2, 3, 4, 1), \
  pi_([2]) & = mat(delim: "(", 1, 2, 3, 4; 3, 4, 1, 2), quad && pi_([3]) = mat(delim: "(", 1, 2, 3, 4; 4, 1, 2, 3)
$
$ H = {pi_e, pi_([1]), pi_([2]), pi_([3])} subset S_4, quad H tilde.equiv ZZ_4 $

- *Cycles:* special $pi in S_n$ which cyclically permute subsets of $S = {1, ..., n}$:
  $
    S_n in.rev (s_0, ..., s_(k-1)): quad & s_i |-> s_(i + 1 mod k) \
    & r in S without {s_0, ..., s_(k-1)} |-> r
  $
  $ (1 3 4) in S_4: quad mat(delim: "(", 1, 2, 3, 4; 3, 2, 4, 1) $
  There is a $k$-fold ambiguity in notation: $(s_0, s_1, ..., s_(k-1)) = (s_1, s_2, ..., s_(k-1), s_0)$.

  Non-intersecting cycles commute:
  $ (s_1 ... s_k)(s'_1 ... s'_(k')) = (s'_1 ... s'_(k'))(s_1 ... s_k) quad "s.t." quad {s_1 ... s_k} inter {s'_1 ... s'_(k')} = emptyset $

  *Important fact:* All $pi in S_n$ can be written as a product of non-intersecting cycles!

  _Proof:_ Choose any $pi in S_n$. Define an equivalence relation on $S = {1, ..., n}$:
  $ x tilde y quad <=> quad exists l in NN_0: y = pi^l (x), quad Gamma_pi tilde.equiv ZZ_k "for some" k $
  $
    S & = union.dot.big "equivalence classes" = union.dot.big "orbits under" Gamma_pi \
    S & = S^0 union.dot S^1 union.dot dots union.dot S^m, quad S^i = {s_i, pi(s_i), pi^2 (s_i), ..., pi^(n_i - 1) (s_i)}, quad n_i := |S^i|
  $
  $ pi = underbrace((s_1, pi(s_1), ..., pi^(n_1 - 1) (s_1))(s_2, pi(s_2), ...)(s_3, ...) dots (s_m, pi(s_m), ..., pi^(n_m - 1) (s_m)), "product of non-intersecting cycles") $

  *Example:*
  $ pi = mat(delim: "(", 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7; 4, 1, 6, 2, 5, 3, 7) $
  $ S = {1, 4, 2} union.dot {3, 6} union.dot {5} union.dot {7}, quad pi = (1, 4, 2)(3, 6) $

*Fact:* Under conjugation, cycles of a particular length go to cycles of the same length.
$ sigma in S_n, quad (s_1, ..., s_k) in S_n, quad sigma (s_1, ..., s_k) sigma^(-1) = (sigma(s_1), ..., sigma(s_k)) $
_Proof:_
$
  sigma (s_1 ... s_k) sigma^(-1) (sigma(s_i)) & = sigma (s_1, ..., s_k)(s_i) = sigma(s_(i+1)) \
  => sigma (s_1, ..., s_k) sigma^(-1) & = (sigma(s_1) ... sigma(s_k)) #h(4em) square
$

The conjugacy class is determined by the length structure of the cyclic decomposition.

Conjugacy classes are characterized by the _cycle structure_ $(k_1, ..., k_n)$, where $k_i >= 0$ is the number of cycles of length $i$,
$ sum_i i k_i = n, $
$
  |C_((k_1, ..., k_n))| = overbrace(n!, "all permutations")/(product_i underbrace(i^(k_i), #stack(spacing: 3pt, [cyclic permutations], [in cycles])) underbrace(k_i !, #stack(spacing: 3pt, [permutations of cycles], [of same length]))),
$
where e.g. $(s_1 s_2 s_3) = (s_2 s_3 s_1)$ ($i$: length of the cycle, $k_i$: number of cycles with length $i$).

_Reparametrisation:_ $(k_1, ..., k_n) |-> (lambda_1, ..., lambda_n)$,
$ lambda_i = sum_(j=i)^n k_j quad #aside[($lambda_i$ is the number of cycles of length at least $i$)] $
$ k_i = lambda_i - lambda_(i+1), quad sum_(i=1)^n lambda_i = sum_(i=1)^n sum_(j=i)^n k_j = sum_j j k_j = n $
$(lambda_1, ..., lambda_n)$ is a partition of $n$.

Characterize conjugacy classes:
$
  "cycle structures" & -> {(k_1, ..., k_n) | k_i >= 0, sum i k_i = n} \
  & #h(5em) arrow.t.b "1:1" \
  "partitions of" n & -> {(lambda_1, ..., lambda_n) | lambda_1 >= lambda_2 >= ... >= lambda_n >= 0, sum lambda_i = n} \
  & #h(5em) arrow.t.b \
  & #h(3.2em) "conjugacy classes of" S_n
$
#aside[Nice way to visualise partitions by means of _Young diagrams_.]

The _Young diagram_ associated to $(lambda_1, ..., lambda_k)$ is a picture of $n$ boxes in columns of heights $lambda_i$:
#grid(
  columns: (auto, auto),
  column-gutter: 2em,
  align: horizon,
  young((5, 4, none, 0), labels: ($lambda_1$, $lambda_2$, none, $lambda_k$)),
  [$square$ #sym.tilde.op $n$ boxes all together],
)

*Example:* $S_8$: $(dot.c dot.c dot.c)(dot.c dot.c)(dot.c dot.c)(dot.c)$
#grid(
  columns: (auto, auto, auto),
  column-gutter: 3em,
  align: horizon,
  $ k_3 & = 1 \ k_2 & = 2 \ k_1 & = 1 $,
  $ lambda_1 & = 4 \ lambda_2 & = 3 \ lambda_3 & = 1 $,
  young((4, 3, 1)),
)

\# conjugacy classes of $S_n$ = \# of partitions of $n$ $= p(n)$:
$
  p(1) & = 1 \
  p(2) & = 2, quad && 2 = 2 + 0, 1 + 1 \
  p(3) & = 3, quad && 3 = 3 + 0, 2 + 1, 1 + 1 + 1 \
  p(4) & = 5 \
  p(5) & = 7 \
  & dots.v
$
Generating functions:
$ product_(k >= 1) (1/(1 - x^k)) = product_k sum_(i >= 0) x^(k i) = sum_n x^n p(n), $
$ (1 + x + x^2 + dots)(1 + x^2 + x^4 + x^6 + dots)(1 + x^3 + x^6 + x^9 + dots) dots $

- *Specific cycles:*
  - _transpositions:_
    $
      sigma_i = (i, i + 1): quad cases(i |-> i + 1, i + 1 |-> i, r |-> r\, quad r != i\, i + 1) quad quad sigma_i sigma_i = e
    $
    $S_n$ is generated by transpositions (all $pi in S_n$ can be written as products of transpositions).

    _Proof:_ Take $pi in S_n$, $pi(n) = i$:
    $ tilde(pi) = sigma_(n-1) sigma_(n-2) dots sigma_i pi, quad tilde(pi)(n) = n. $
    If $tilde(pi)$ is a product of transpositions, then so is $pi = sigma_i sigma_(i+1) dots sigma_(n-1) tilde(pi)$. \
    $S_2 = {e, (1 2)}$ is generated by transpositions.

    $ "length"(pi) = min{k | pi = sigma_(i_1) dots sigma_(i_k)} $

    Define $phi: S_n -> ZZ_2$:
    $
      p(x_1, ..., x_n) & = product_(i < j) (x_i - x_j), \
      phi(pi) & = (p(x_(pi(1)), ..., x_(pi(n))))/(p(x_1, ..., x_n)) in {plus.minus 1}, \
      phi(pi sigma) & = (p(x_(pi sigma(1)), ..., x_(pi sigma(n))))/(p(x_1, ..., x_n)) = underbrace((p(x_(pi sigma(1)), ..., x_(pi sigma(n))))/(p(x_(sigma(1)), ..., x_(sigma(n)))), phi(pi)) dot underbrace((p(x_(sigma(1)), ..., x_(sigma(n))))/(p(x_1, ..., x_n)), phi(sigma)),
    $
    $
      phi(sigma_i) & = -1, quad phi(pi) = (-1)^("length"(pi)), \
      ker(phi) & = {pi | phi(pi) = 1} = {pi "of even length"} lt.closed S_n
    $
    $A_n$: _alternating group_, $S_n\/A_n tilde.equiv ZZ_2$.

*Remark:* Finite (simple) groups have been classified:
- cyclic groups $ZZ_p$
- alternating groups
- simple groups of Lie type
- one of 26 sporadic groups (Monster)
